  "query_preview": "through Friday and now includes the entire area. The \noppressive heat has maintained today over most of the area as \nboundary layer humidity is peaking at the worst time in \nassociation with moisture pooling associated with a weak surface \nboundary over our area. High dewpoints combined with temperatures\nrising well into the 90s is sending heat index values to around \n110, but will say that scattered convective precipitation will \nhopefully help to reduce some of the heat, or at least keeping it \nfrom reaching more dangerous levels, as we go through the \nremainder of the afternoon. Expect a general repeat of today's \nweather for Friday with fairly high coverage of diurnally driven \nconvection, and have maintained the elevated heat threat grahic.\n/EC/\n\nSaturday Onwards: The long-term forecast remains on track. \nIncreased chances for convective activity and cloud coverage will \ntake hold due to the surface confluence from the aforementioned \nfront, which could provide a few degree dip, thus mitigating heat \nthreat concerns. With upper flow becoming more northerly and \nnorthwesterly during this time frame, we will see the potential \nfor one or two convective complexes moving into the area or \ndeveloping over the area. This will result in continued higher \ndiurnal rain chances heading into the weekend. Going into early \nnext week, conditions become less favorable for convective\ncoverage. In terms of sensible weather, we'll see rain chances \ndiminish and heat potentially rebuild some, but it should remain \nbelow what we've seen over the last few days. /OAJ/EC/",
